1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,173
India is mapping its land from the sky.

2
00:00:03,553 --> 00:00:07,847
Drones now photograph entire towns at a few centimetres per pixel.

3
00:00:08,227 --> 00:00:10,437
But a photograph is not a land record.

4
00:00:10,817 --> 00:00:15,481
This is GeoParcel AI — our solution to Smart India Hackathon

5
00:00:15,481 --> 00:00:20,068
problem statement 26012: automated urban parcel mapping and

6
00:00:20,068 --> 00:00:23,799
cadastral feature extraction from drone imagery.

7
00:00:25,399 --> 00:00:29,923
Under the NAKSHA programme, the Department of Land Resources is

8
00:00:29,923 --> 00:00:34,519
rebuilding urban land records from aerial surveys, starting with

9
00:00:34,519 --> 00:00:38,253
152 cities across 26 states and 3 union territories.

10
00:00:38,633 --> 00:00:41,933
In rural India, the SVAMITVA scheme has already

11
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:44,742
drone-surveyed around 3.3 lakh villages.

12
00:00:45,122 --> 00:00:48,696
Capturing the imagery is fast. Turning it into maps is not.

13
00:00:49,076 --> 00:00:52,320
Every building outline and parcel boundary still

14
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:55,090
has to be traced by hand in GIS software.

15
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:58,746
A single scene of about three by three kilometres in

16
00:00:58,746 --> 00:01:01,706
our dataset contains more than 8,000 buildings.

17
00:01:02,086 --> 00:01:05,771
Across a whole city, manual digitisation becomes the bottleneck

18
00:01:05,771 --> 00:01:09,221
between the drone flight and the citizen's property record.

19
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:12,900
Automating this is harder than it looks.

20
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:17,610
In dense neighbourhoods buildings share walls, trees and shadows hide

21
00:01:17,610 --> 00:01:21,313
roof edges, and roofing ranges from tin sheets to concrete.

22
00:01:21,693 --> 00:01:26,128
And even a good AI model outputs pixels, not parcels — jagged blobs

23
00:01:26,128 --> 00:01:30,100
with hundreds of vertices that no surveyor can use directly.

24
00:01:30,480 --> 00:01:34,600
A useful system has to deliver clean, georeferenced polygons.

25
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:37,382
Here is our pipeline.

26
00:01:37,762 --> 00:01:42,604
First, the drone orthomosaic and its reference labels are aligned

27
00:01:42,604 --> 00:01:46,924
in one map coordinate system and cut into 512-pixel tiles.

28
00:01:47,304 --> 00:01:51,308
That sounds trivial, but it isn't: the labels are stored in

29
00:01:51,308 --> 00:01:55,381
latitude and longitude, the imagery in UTM metres — skip the

30
00:01:55,381 --> 00:01:59,046
reprojection, and every training mask comes out empty.

31
00:01:59,426 --> 00:02:03,305
Second, a U-Net segmentation network with a ResNet-34

32
00:02:03,305 --> 00:02:07,476
encoder classifies every pixel as building or background.

33
00:02:07,856 --> 00:02:10,735
Third, we trace polygons from the predicted mask.

34
00:02:11,115 --> 00:02:14,607
Fourth — the key step — a regularisation stage turns

35
00:02:14,607 --> 00:02:17,830
jagged outlines into clean, right-angled shapes.

36
00:02:18,210 --> 00:02:23,748
Finally, the polygons are mapped back to real-world coordinates and exported as

37
00:02:23,748 --> 00:02:29,426
GeoJSON parcel candidates, ready for a Web-GIS and for verification in the field.

38
00:02:31,026 --> 00:02:34,750
Here is that regularisation step on a real validation tile.

39
00:02:35,130 --> 00:02:40,863
The raw contour of this building follows every pixel step: 463 vertices.

40
00:02:41,243 --> 00:02:44,955
Our regulariser cleans the mask, simplifies the outline,

41
00:02:44,955 --> 00:02:48,269
and estimates the building's dominant orientation.

42
00:02:48,649 --> 00:02:52,619
Every edge close to that orientation, or perpendicular to it, is

43
00:02:52,619 --> 00:02:56,650
refitted as a straight line through the original boundary pixels,

44
00:02:56,650 --> 00:02:59,813
and the corners are rebuilt where those lines meet.

45
00:03:00,193 --> 00:03:04,304
The result: 5 vertices — a shape a surveyor can actually work with.

46
00:03:06,105 --> 00:03:07,937
Here is the working prototype.

47
00:03:09,137 --> 00:03:12,298
We upload a drone tile, and the trained model runs on it.

48
00:03:13,898 --> 00:03:17,728
Moments later, we see the input, the raw segmentation mask,

49
00:03:17,728 --> 00:03:20,975
and the regularised polygons drawn over the image.

50
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:27,109
The metrics panel compares the two outputs: the polygon count, and

51
00:03:27,109 --> 00:03:31,270
the average vertex count, which drops from about 257 to 6.

52
00:03:32,470 --> 00:03:38,499
With a ground-truth mask uploaded, the app also scores pixel IoU and polygon IoU.

53
00:03:39,899 --> 00:03:43,007
And the prediction threshold can be tuned from the sidebar.

54
00:03:44,807 --> 00:03:45,804
Now, the numbers.

55
00:03:46,184 --> 00:03:52,317
We trained on one scene from the Open Cities AI Challenge — real drone imagery at

56
00:03:52,317 --> 00:03:57,694
7.2 centimetres per pixel — using about 5,000 tiles on a single laptop.

57
00:03:58,074 --> 00:04:02,524
Validation IoU climbed over 10 epochs to 0.63.

58
00:04:02,904 --> 00:04:08,616
On 502 held-out tiles, the pixel IoU is 0.60, and regularisation

59
00:04:08,616 --> 00:04:14,149
cuts polygon complexity about 27 times and roughly doubles the

60
00:04:14,149 --> 00:04:19,504
share of right-angle corners, while polygon overlap with the

61
00:04:19,504 --> 00:04:23,966
ground truth stays close: 0.47 before, 0.46 after.

62
00:04:24,346 --> 00:04:26,711
Then we ran the complete pipeline on a dense

63
00:04:26,711 --> 00:04:29,507
neighbourhood from a scene the model had never seen.

64
00:04:29,887 --> 00:04:34,598
It reached a pixel IoU of 0.80 and produced 427

65
00:04:34,598 --> 00:04:39,410
parcel candidates, exported directly as GeoJSON.

66
00:04:39,790 --> 00:04:43,692
In the densest blocks, buildings that share walls can merge into a

67
00:04:43,692 --> 00:04:47,476
single candidate — one more reason a surveyor stays in the loop.

68
00:04:49,275 --> 00:04:51,120
We want to be clear about what this is.

69
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:55,646
These are preliminary parcel candidates for field verification: a

70
00:04:55,646 --> 00:05:00,174
decision-support tool that takes over the repetitive tracing, while the

71
00:05:00,174 --> 00:05:04,255
surveyor keeps the final say — not an automated legal judgement.

72
00:05:04,635 --> 00:05:09,562
Next, we plan to fine-tune on Indian NAKSHA and SVAMITVA imagery, extend from

73
00:05:09,562 --> 00:05:14,745
building footprints to plot boundaries such as walls, fences and roads, and close

74
00:05:14,745 --> 00:05:19,159
the loop so that every surveyor correction becomes new training data.

75
00:05:20,759 --> 00:05:22,070
GeoParcel AI.

76
00:05:22,450 --> 00:05:25,073
From drone pixels to parcel candidates —

77
00:05:25,073 --> 00:05:28,155
faster, consistent, and ready for verification.

78
00:05:28,535 --> 00:05:29,271
Thank you.

